\title{Identity Types}
\taxon{Type Theory}
\date{2024-01-31}
\import{macros}
\p{
    This note discusses identity types in type theory.
    Refer to [Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory](hott-2022).
}
\p{
    How can we think of \strong{equality} in type theory?
    Given a type #{A} and two elements #{a,b:A},
    we can define a new type #{a=_Ab} which is called the \strong{identity type}.
    a term of #{a=_Ab} is a \em{witness} identifying #{a} and #{b}.
    The witness is a term of an identity type, and two such witnesses can themselves be compared
    by another identity type.
    This gives every type a \strong{groupoid structure}.
}
\transclude{def-002N}
\subtree{
    \title{The groupoid structure of types}
    \p{
        The identifications can be \strong{concatenated} and \strong{inverted},
        which implies the \em{transitivity} and \em{symmetry} of the identity type.
    }
    \transclude{def-002O}
    \transclude{def-002P}
    \p{
        It is natural to ask whether the identity type is \em{reflexive}.
        In the case of associativity we should ask to compare the identifications:
        ##{
            (p\cdot q)\cdot r \quad\text{and}\quad p\cdot(q\cdot r)
        }
        for any #{p:x=y}, #{q:y=z} and #{r:z=w} in a type #{A}. Both #{ (p\cdot q)\cdot r} and 
        #{p\cdot(q\cdot r)} are elements of type #{x=w}. We can ask whether there is an
        identification
        ##{
            (p\cdot q)\cdot r = p\cdot(q\cdot r)
        }
    }
    \transclude{def-002W}
    \transclude{def-002X}
}
\subtree{
    \title{The action on identifications of functions}
    \p{
        Every function preserves identifications.
    }
    \transclude{def-004C}
    \p{
        For a dependent function #{f:\Pi_{(x:A)}B(x)}, its dependent action on paths requires
        \strong{transport}, because the two endpoint values live in potentially different fibers.
        Type theory cannot directly compare terms from different types, even when their indices are identified.
    }
    \transclude{def-004D}
}
\subtree{
    \title{Uniqueness of refl}
    \p{
        The identity type is an inductive family of types.
        For instance, while the type #{a=x} indexed by #{x:A} is inductively generated by #{\refl_a},
        the type #{a=a} is \strong{not}, because the endpoint of #{p:a=a} is not free. 
        In general, we cannot show that #{p=\refl_a} for every #{p:a=a}.
    }
    \p{
        Nevertheless the identity type #{a=x} is generated by a single element #{\refl_a:a=a},
        so it is natural to ask in \em{what sense} the reflexivity identification is unique.
        The correct statement is that the pair #{(a,\refl_a)} is the center of contraction of
        the singleton type
        ##{
            (x,p):\Sigma_(x:A) a = x 
        }
        That is, for every #{y:\Sigma_(x:A)(a=x)}, there is an identification
        #{(a,\refl_a)=y}.
    }
    \proof{
        By #{\Sigma} induction it suffices to show that there is an identification
        ##{
            (a, \refl_a) = (x, p)
        }
        for every #{x:A} and #{p:a=x}. Then apply the induction principle of identity types.
        It suffices to show that
        ##{
            (a,\refl_a) = (a, \refl_a)
        }
        which can be obtained by reflexivity.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{The laws of addition on #{\mathbb{N}}}
    \p{
        The computation rules for addition give judgmental equalities
        #{m+0\equiv m} and #{m+\succ(n)\equiv\succ(m+n)}. The laws in the other
        argument are propositional equalities and must be proved by induction:
        ##{
            0+n=n,\qquad \succ(m)+n=\succ(m+n).
        }
        The inductive step applies #{\succ} to the induction hypothesis using the action on paths.
    }
    \p{
        These four unit and successor laws are the reusable lemmas needed for the familiar
        algebraic laws. Induction on the final argument proves associativity,
        ##{
            (m+n)+k=m+(n+k),
        }
        and induction together with both successor laws proves commutativity,
        #{m+n=n+m}. The distinction matters: computation supplies only judgmental equality;
        the remaining equations are inhabitants of identity types.
    }
}
